WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.782
Once trained,

00:00:04.862 --> 00:00:09.644
SKiNN produces a velocity image from an input
parameter vector in about fifty milliseconds on a single GPU,

00:00:09.724 --> 00:00:14.506
which is roughly three hundred times faster than JAM.

00:00:14.586 --> 00:00:19.368
Accuracy is assessed on the five hundred held-out
test images by computing the relative error at each pixel.

00:00:19.448 --> 00:00:24.230
While some outer regions can show errors of a few percent,

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:29.092
the important innermost region, where real data
actually constrains the model,

00:00:29.172 --> 00:00:33.954
is matched to within plus or minus one percent for
almost all pixels.

00:00:34.034 --> 00:00:38.816
Averaged over entire images,

00:00:38.896 --> 00:00:43.678
the median absolute error is about 0.47 percent and
the ninetieth percentile is about 1.1 percent.

00:00:43.758 --> 00:00:48.540
Both are comfortably below the typical
two-percent-or-greater systematic uncertainty in real velocity measurements.
